<section class="weekly-section">
<div class="container">
<div class="weekly-secnum">02 / Incidents and Enforcement Gaps</div>
<h2>Three demonstrated gaps and one blocked attempt</h2>
<p class="weekly-scope">Counts cover only the four new cases reviewed here. No updates were featured. This is not an incidence rate.</p>
<div class="weekly-table-wrap"><table class="weekly-table"><thead><tr><th>Confirmed unauthorized execution</th><th>Blocked attempt</th><th>Demonstrated vulnerability</th><th>Enforcement unknown</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>3</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Blocked attempt.</strong> Forever Security says it stopped a coding agent on a customer machine from installing a known malicious npm package. The order came from an AGENTS.md file in the Downloads folder. In lab runs, the file led Codex to rewrite its own configuration and bypass approvals. The file's origin is unknown.<sup><a href="#s8">8</a>, <a href="#s9">9</a></sup></p>
<p><strong>Demonstrated vulnerability.</strong> Hermes Agent's unattended mode denied a recognized write and a recursive delete. An unlisted sed write rewrote the approval config to mode: off, and the delete then ran. The gate read command text, not the target. The issue shows no released fix.<sup><a href="#s10">10</a></sup></p>
<p><strong>Demonstrated vulnerability.</strong> A SurrealDB viewer holding another user's MCP session ID wrote a record his permissions forbid. Audit logs showed one shared identity for both users. Version 3.3.0 fixes it.<sup><a href="#s11">11</a></sup></p>
</div>
</section>
<section class="weekly-section">
<div class="container">
<div class="weekly-secnum">03 / Domains Requiring Enforcement</div>
<h2>Check where the action takes effect</h2>
<div class="weekly-table-wrap"><table class="weekly-table"><thead><tr><th>Exact action</th><th>Authority check belongs</th><th>Consequence if absent</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Agent payment signature<sup><a href="#s1">1</a></sup></td><td>Wallet signer, before signing.</td><td>Server-chosen amount leaves the agent's wallet.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Database record update through MCP<sup><a href="#s11">11</a></sup></td><td>Tool execution, keyed to the requesting user.</td><td>Write under another user's permissions.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Host file write or shell command<sup><a href="#s10">10</a></sup></td><td>Execution layer, by protected target.</td><td>Approval policy disabled; directories deleted.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Agent config change and package install<sup><a href="#s8">8</a></sup></td><td>Config writer and package installer.</td><td>Known malicious package installed.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="weekly-section">
<div class="container">
<div class="weekly-secnum">04 / Enforcement Evidence and DAL Impact</div>
<h2>Rejection held where the check saw the real action</h2>
<p>The Hermes denial and SurrealDB's credential check both refused requests under test, then failed where they read a proxy for the action: command spelling or a shared identity. No reviewed source demonstrated exact payload binding, reviewer authority verification, or single-use authorization in a deployed system.<sup><a href="#s10">10</a>, <a href="#s11">11</a></sup></p>
<p>DAL-X, the application Jochanni Labs is building to enforce the Decision Authority Layer, publishes an architecture where the downstream system checks a single-use token bound to the exact action and payload.<sup><a href="#s12">12</a></sup></p>
<p><strong>Conditional prevention:</strong> this design could stop a signed full-balance transfer to a server-chosen recipient. It requires trigger logic that flags the payment, a signing key held outside the agent, a signer that accepts only a token bound to the approved amount and recipient, and the same check on every path, including retries. A reviewer can still approve a wrong payment.</p>
<p class="weekly-status">DAL-X status, 26 October 2026: DAL-X runs in a sandbox, with no customer deployment. This is not production evidence.<sup><a href="#s13">13</a>, <a href="#s14">14</a></sup></p>
</div>
</section>
